\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces COO (coordinate) format. Each non-zero is stored as a (\texttt {row}, \texttt {col}, \texttt {value}) triple, so the $k$-th entry of the three arrays describes the $k$-th non-zero.}}{5}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces CSR (compressed sparse row) format. \texttt {values} and \texttt {col\_indices} hold the non-zero values and their column indices, row by row. Row $i$ occupies positions \texttt {row\_ptr[i]} to \texttt {row\_ptr[i+1]-1}; e.g.\ row 3 spans positions 4 to 6.}}{5}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces CSC (compressed sparse column) format. The non-zeros are stored column by column; \texttt {row\_indices} holds their row indices. Column $j$ occupies positions \texttt {col\_ptr[j]} to \texttt {col\_ptr[j+1]-1}; e.g.\ column 2 spans positions 3 to 4.}}{6}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces Inner-product dataflow. One output element $c_{ij}$ is produced at a time by intersecting row $i$ of $A$ with column $j$ of $B$ and summing the matching products. Each output is complete when it is written, but most intersections are empty for sparse inputs, so effort is spent on index matching.}}{8}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces Outer-product dataflow. For each index $k$, column $k$ of $A$ is multiplied with row $k$ of $B$, producing a partial result that touches many positions of $C$. Every input element is read once, but the partial results from different $k$ must be merged.}}{9}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces Row-wise (Gustavson) dataflow. Each non-zero $a_{ik}$ in row $i$ of $A$ selects row $k$ of $B$. The scaled rows are accumulated into row $i$ of $C$. One output row is built at a time, so the accumulator for that row could potentially be kept in cache.}}{9}{}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces One matrix in three formats (indices from zero). In CSR, \texttt {pos[i]} to \texttt {pos[i+1]} delimits row $i$ in \texttt {crd}; row~1 is empty, so \texttt {pos[1]}$=$\texttt {pos[2]}. CSC is the same over columns. COO repeats the row coordinate per non-zero and has no \texttt {pos}.}}{15}{}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Observed and Formula FLOPs. Observed data points fall mostly on the dotted line plotted from formulas.}}{42}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Observed and Formula memops. Observed data points fall mostly on the dotted line plotted from formulas.}}{46}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces FLOPs: Relative error of Formula vs Observed. Very close fit by the formula, and flop count is determined entirely by compression combination.}}{47}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces memops: Relative error of Formula vs Observed. Close fit by most formulas, variability due to the effect of variability of sparsity patterns.}}{48}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Runtime Performance: Timestamp Cycles. Crossover points at 0.7\% density when performing a simple dense x dense is better.}}{49}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Roofline Model. Sparse formats are entirely memory bound.}}{50}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Diagonal Matrix Results. Poor performance compared to simple global compression, but optimal micro-format selection wins over naive format selection.}}{51}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Block Size Performance Impact. Performance degrades as block sizes increase.}}{52}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Thread Count Performance Impact. At matrix sizes that can be solved, threading provides minimal benefit beyond 2 threads.}}{53}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces Kronecker Graph Sweep (AS-Newman). Sparse BLIS performs very poorly compared to a global compression strategy, this worsens as matrix size increases.}}{54}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces Kronecker Graph k=10 (AS-Newman) block size sweep. Block dispatch overhead generally decreases as block size increases, but not nearly enough to be comparable to simple global compression.}}{55}{}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces This figure shows the best performing format combo for each of the studied graphs at each K. Each square is also labeled with a speedup over runner up (e.g. 1.1x). There is a broad trend of the best being CSR $\times $ CSR at smaller K, then transitioning to CSC $\times $ CSR or CSC $\times $ COO at larger K. In most cases the difference between CSC $\times $ CSR beating CSC $\times $ COO or vice versa could potentially be contributed to noise as often they are runner ups of each other within 1.1x. 1 Outlier exists with ATP-GR-QC where CSC $\times $ COO beats CSC $\times $ CSR by an average 1.5x. }}{64}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces This figure shows the best performing format combo for each of the studied graphs at increasing density buckets. Each bucket collects densities in a range 0.5 decade wide. Again, there is a broad trend of the best being CSR $\times $ CSR at higher density, then transitioning to CSC $\times $ CSR or CSC $\times $ COO for sparser matrices. That this fits pretty well with K makes sense since Kroneckers become increasingly sparse as K increases. }}{67}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces This figure shows the best performing format combo for each of the studied graphs at increasing nnz. Here the broad transition from CSC $\times $ CSR, to the other formats is much starker. Around ~$10^3$ non-zeroes CSR $\times $ CSR stops being the best for nearly all datasets. }}{68}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Cost of always using one pair, relative to the per-point best, all graphs together.}}{68}{}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces Size of block plotted against $k$. Almost always, the best tiling is a single tile at the matrix dimensions, or in other words, no tiling at all.}}{69}{}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
